; =====================================================================
; canonical.abnf
;
; VERBATIM -- this is RFC 9804's own grammar, copied unchanged.
; It was NOT authored by this project. Do not edit it, do not correct
; it, do not reformat it. Defects found in it are recorded in
; README.md, never repaired here.
;
; Locator:  RFC 9804, "Simple Public Key Infrastructure (SPKI)
;           S-Expressions", Rivest & Eastlake, June 2025,
;           Informational, ISSN 2070-1721.
;           Section 7.2, "ABNF for Canonical".
;           Source text lines 777-779.
;           <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9804.txt>
;
; Source digest (sha256 of the full source text):
;   0db36cc4bb9d0e08c4999ab775c955daec0f62e849bbfe688e223998d5a44434
;
; Verbatim region digest (sha256 of the bytes between the BEGIN and END
; markers below, markers excluded):
;   de8ceda94f48c0fef515c8c1afb7762b13d12d39d94622e6eb0931f5751f09ba
;
; The region preserves the RFC's three-space left margin and its
; internal blank line. Byte-exactness was confirmed by diffing the
; extracted region against the source lines, not by reading.
;
; The BEGIN/END markers are written as ABNF comments so that this file
; remains machine-checkable as ABNF (RFC 5234).
;
; NOT SELF-CONTAINED. These two rules reference "verbatim", which is
; defined only in Section 7.1 (advanced-transport.abnf), and which in
; turn pulls in "decimal" and the OCTET core rule. An ABNF tool fed
; this file alone reports "verbatim" undefined. This is the RFC's own
; structure -- Section 7 says the canonical form is "derived" from the
; advanced one -- and nothing has been added here to close it.
;
; Start symbol: c-sexp.
; =====================================================================

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM ----
   c-sexp         =  c-string / ("(" *c-sexp ")")

   c-string       =  [ "[" verbatim "]" ] verbatim
; ---- END VERBATIM ----
